WEBVTT
TITLE Floating Point Arithmetic — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
The last lesson turned bits into numbers. This one operates on them: align, add, normalise, round.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A format is a promise about spacing: with four significand bits, numbers near one are one-eighth apart.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The page computes in a toy format: four significand bits, exponents from minus three to three.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
IEEE promises one rounding per operation, applied to the exact result. That promise is the whole design.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
To add two numbers you must first put them on the same exponent: shift the smaller one right.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
One plus one-sixteenth in four bits: the difference is four, so four bits are shifted out.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The result is one, not 1.0625: the addend was absorbed, and rounding put the sum back on one.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
One plus one-eighth is 1.125 exactly: the same shape of question, a different exponent gap.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Rounding asks one question: which of the two neighbours is nearer?

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
1.0625 is a tie, and ties go to the even last digit, so it becomes 1.000 and not 1.125.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Above the halfway point it goes up: 1.09375 becomes 1.125. Below it, down: 1.03125 becomes 1.000.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Five modes, one question: nearest ties to even, nearest ties away, toward zero, toward plus infinity, toward minus infinity.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Two minus 1.75 in three significand bits: align, and the last bit of 1.75 falls off the end.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Without a guard bit the answer is 0.5. The true answer is 0.25, so the error is one hundred per cent.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
One guard bit is enough: with it the answer is 0.25 exactly, and the error is zero.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The sticky bit is the memory of everything shifted out: one if any of it was non-zero.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
One tenth in binary is a repeating fraction, so the stored double is not one tenth.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The two stored doubles add to a value one unit in the last place above the double nearest 0.3.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The residue is exactly two to the minus fifty-four: one bit, not a rounding accident.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
In single precision the same three numbers are stored with only 24 bits, and the printed digits change.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Ten to the eight plus one, in single precision, is ten to the eight: the one has nowhere to go.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Subtracting ten to the eight then gives zero, although the true answer is one.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The quadratic formula has the same disease: the small root of x squared plus ten to the eight x plus one.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The naive formula gives minus 7.45 times ten to the minus nine; the rearranged form gives the true minus ten to the minus eight.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
GATE 2004 asked this exact question in three-digit decimal arithmetic: two groupings, two answers.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Left grouping: 113 minus 111 is 2, and 2 plus 7.51 is 9.51. Nothing was lost.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Right grouping: minus 111 plus 7.51 needs three digits to hold 103, so 7.51 is first cut to 8.00.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
In double precision the same non-associativity survives: 0.6000000000000001 against 0.6.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Multiplication has no alignment step: multiply the significands and add the exponent fields.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Minus sixteen times 0.86009145: exponent fields 131 and 126 add to 257, minus 127 is 130, and the product is minus 13.761463.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Division subtracts the fields and adds the bias back; the significands divide and are rounded once.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
At the ends of the range the answer is infinity or zero: ten to the thirty-eight times ten overflows a single.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Twenty-four significand bits is about seven decimal digits; fifty-three bits is about sixteen.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Every integer up to two to the twenty-four is exact in a float, and 16777217 is not one of them.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
GATE 2025 Set 2 gave three registers: minus 9, 6 and 12. GATE 2026 Set 1 gave a sum.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Equality between floats is a question about bits, so compare with a tolerance and say what the tolerance is.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: write both operands with their exponent and significand, and subtract the exponents.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: shift, add, and keep a guard and a sticky bit; step three: normalise and round once.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step four: re-encode and check the regime — normal, denormal, infinity, not-a-number.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The four traps: bias 127 not 128, the hidden one, one rounding per operation, and the sign of the exponent.

